  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 204 PM MDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nMain upper trough moves onshore Sunday morning, pushing into the\nGreat Basin by Sunday evening, then across Colorado on Mon.\nAhead of the trough, moist sw flow will develop across swrn CO,\nwith rain starting in the ern San Juans shortly after sunrise \nSun, becoming heavy from midday into the evening, then changing\nto snow overnight into Mon morning as colder air aloft under the\nupper trough moves across and mid-level flow becomes more wly.\nModel QPF in the San Juans continues to rise, with forecast\namounts of over 3 inches of liquid near Wolf Creek Pass by Mon\nevening. Snow level rises to above 10000 feet by Sun afternoon,\nbefore falling back toward 9000 feet early Mon morning, which\nsuggests the threat of several hours of rain on portions of the\nSan Juan snowpack (SWE 1-3 inches below about 11k feet), which\nwill increase the flooding risk. Did issue a Winter storm watch\nfor Sun night into midday Mon for the higher elevation San Juan\nzone, where over a foot of snow is possible. Elsewhere, lighter\nshowers spread across the higher terrain during the day Sun \ninto Sun night, though precip amounts away from the San Juans \nwill be much lighter, generally less than a half inch. Plains \nwill stay warm and dry again on Sun, with increasing s-sw winds \nas surface low spins up near Limon. \n\nOn Mon, upper trough moves across CO, as upper low lifts out of\nthe ern plains into ern KS by Mon evening. Showers will continue\nover the mountains through the day, with lowering snow levels at\nmost locations as cold air deepens under the trough. Plains \nwill see wrap-around showers and strong north winds as low\ndeparts, with heaviest precip along/south of the Arkansas River\nwhere nly upslope component will boost vertical motion. Precip\nends Mon night, though north winds will remain gusty on the\nplains into the first half of the day Tue. Flat upper ridge then\nreturns for most of the week from Tue onward, with warm and dry\nweather most locations, though next upper trough and precip are\nalready lining up for next weekend.",
